## Windows host: gate B failed on the harness, not the code (2026-09-02, laptop round 1)

`gate.txt` from the owner's laptop: `reached: B; result: FAIL; first failure: Gate B — 5 parity tests
fail with [WinError 193] esbuild not a valid Win32 application (host gap)`. Evidence commit `2b1221a4`.
Gate A -- the strict scoped typecheck, the only lane that proves the registration satisfies
`IChatSessionItemController` / `IChatSessionItem` / `ChatSessionStatus` / `sessionOpenerRegistry` --
**passed on Windows**.

Cause, owned here: `pulse-webview\node_modules\.bin\esbuild` on that machine is a POSIX shim from a
Linux-installed tree, and my lane only asked whether the file *exists*. The lane now picks its loader at
run time: esbuild when `esbuild --version` actually executes, otherwise `typescript/bin/tsc`, which is
plain JS and emits the module's whole import closure as ESM. tsc exits non-zero on the base/ type
errors this checkout cannot resolve, so success is measured by the file it wrote.

Re-verified in the sandbox by simulating the host (esbuild present, non-executable):

```
$ .venv/bin/python -m pytest src/tests/test_pulse_session_registration_parity.py -q -s   # esbuild shimmed dead
8 passed            # tsc route, same fixtures, same assertions
$ .venv/bin/python -m pytest ...parity.py test_hermes_activity_parity.py test_desktop_renderer_architecture.py test_desktop_contrib_overlay.py -q
31 passed, 2 skipped, 1 xfailed        # esbuild route restored
$ tsc -p src/tsconfig.pulseai-check.json --noEmit | grep -c '^vs/workbench/contrib/pulseai'
Pulse errors: 0
```

## Windows host, round 3: the parity lane decoded node's UTF-8 as cp1252 (mine, not his)

His evidence commit `76a5e77a`: gate B again red, this time **4** tests, and the loader fix had already
taken effect -- the failures were value mismatches, not `WinError 193`. Root cause was still mine:
`subprocess.run(..., text=True)` decodes with the ambient locale, which on a Windows console is cp1252,
so the ellipsis in `Working…` arrived as mojibake and the honest comparison failed. The projection was
innocent in both rounds.

Fix in all three node-executing parity files: an explicit `_node_json()` that captures **bytes** and
decodes UTF-8 itself, plus `encoding="utf-8", errors="replace"` on the loader probes so even a failure
message cannot crash on undecodable bytes. Verified against his exact combination -- esbuild shimmed
non-executable (forcing the tsc route) *and* a hostile locale:

```
$ env LC_ALL=C PYTHONUTF8=0 PYTHONIOENCODING=ascii pytest test_pulse_session_registration_parity.py -q
8 passed          # tsc loader, ascii locale
$ pytest <the 7 desktop/branding/parity lanes> -q
57 passed, 3 skipped, 1 xfailed
```

Reproduced first, so the diagnosis is not a guess: the old `text=True` pattern under `LC_ALL=C` raises
`UnicodeDecodeError: 'ascii' codec can't decode byte 0xe2`, and under cp1252 it decodes *successfully
into wrong characters* -- which is why his run showed failures rather than a crash.

## Windows host, round 4: the same shim assumption in the two Hermes lanes (evidence `eadf5634`)

His report separated the two facts cleanly: **registration parity 9/9 green** (the UTF-8 fix worked, and
that lane resolves its loader), while 6 Hermes-parity tests died on `[WinError 193]` because
`test_hermes_activity_parity.py` / `test_hermes_run_summary_parity.py` still hardcoded
`node_modules/.bin/esbuild`. I had edited both files the same round for the encoding bug and left the
assumption two lines away -- the fix was incomplete, not the diagnosis wrong.

`src/tests/_node_loader.py` now owns loader resolution for all three lanes (probe `.bin/esbuild`,
`.bin/esbuild.cmd`, `@esbuild/<platform>/bin/esbuild[.exe]`, `@esbuild/<platform>/esbuild[.exe]`,
`esbuild/bin/esbuild`; a candidate must answer `--version`). Verified by becoming his host: shim replaced
by a non-executable script -> resolver picked `@esbuild/linux-x64/bin/esbuild`, 20 passed / 1 skipped;
platform package removed as well -> fell through to `esbuild/bin/esbuild`, 58 passed / 3 skipped /
1 xfailed. A source-level pin refuses any parity lane hardcoding `.bin` again -- and the pin was tested by
reinjecting the hardcode (it failed, naming the file), because the first version of it was red for a
reason worth keeping in mind: a self-scanning pin that spells the pattern literally matches its own
assertion.

## Windows host, round 5 (`e454525c`): his compile caught what my gate A could not see

`TS2459: Module 'agentSessionsOpener.js' declares 'IAgentSession' locally, but it is not exported` at
`pulseAISessionController.ts:28`. He named the one-line fix and correctly declined to apply it under the
stop rules. Applied as his option 1 -- import `IAgentSession` from `agentSessionsModel.js` (where it is
declared and exported, line 126); option 2 was rejected because it edits chat's file to suit Pulse.

**Gate A is why this reached a build at all, and gate A was broken.** Its instruction was
`Select-String -Pattern '^vs/workbench/contrib/pulseai'` run from `desktop\vscode`, where tsc prints
`src/vs/workbench/...`. The anchor could not match, so the count was 0 whatever the tree contained -- on
his host for four rounds and in three of my own runs, including the "0 Pulse errors" claims in this log.
Reproduced in one command after the fix: anchored from `desktop/vscode` -> 0, unanchored -> 1, anchored
from `src` (the directory the tsconfig documents) -> 1.

Two changes so this cannot recur:
* Gate A now runs from `src`, matches `contrib/pulseai` unanchored, and **asserts coverage separately**
  (`Pulse files in program >= 26`), because "no output" and "no errors" are different states and only one
  of them is a pass. An empty log is now a STOP.
* The lane moved into pytest: `test_scoped_typecheck_reports_no_errors_in_our_contribution` runs the same
  config through `typescript/bin/tsc`, asserts our files are in the program (including
  `pulseAISessionController.ts` itself) and that no `contrib/pulseai` line carries an error. Verified in
  both directions -- re-injecting the bad import makes it fail with the TS2459 text; restored, the whole
  python lane is 60 passed / 3 skipped / 1 xfailed, and 11 passed under his host conditions (esbuild shim
  dead, `LC_ALL=C PYTHONUTF8=0`). Correctly-read scoped typecheck on the fixed tree: Pulse errors 0,
  363 environmental.

## Windows host, round 6 (`973a4080`): gate C PASSED -- and the launch bug was mine

`gate.txt`: `reached: D; result: FAIL; first failure: Gate D CDP -- Electron launch: no folder open in
workbench, Pulse composer disabled; doc's arg list format is wrong for this Electron version`, with
**gate C compile PASS**. That is the first real-build proof of the registration: `npm run compile` on the
owner's machine, whole tree, TS2459 gone.

Cause of the D failure, in my doc: I wrote `-ArgumentList '--remote-debugging-port=9222','--user-data-dir',
'D:\path'`. Chromium flags take values with `=`, so the flag was left valueless and the path became a
positional -- read as "what to load", not "the folder to open". No folder, disabled composer, and every
downstream check blaming the UI for a quoting mistake. The operator should not have to know that, so
`scripts/verify_pulse_manager_registration.mjs` now owns it: it builds correct argv (`--user-data-dir=`,
`--disable-extensions`, exactly one positional workspace, defaulting to a temp scratch folder so an open
folder is never optional), waits for the workbench page target, delegates the checks to
`validate_pulse_ui_cdp.js --manager-only`, then evaluates the row query itself and writes
`session-rows.json`. `--dry-run` prints the argv, `--no-launch` attaches to a running IDE, and the
failure paths exit 2/3/4 with the fix in the message. Verified here by dry-run and by both failure paths;
the launch itself needs Electron, which only his host has.

Two pins added, one of them after its own bug: the first version asserted the broken form was absent from
the docs and failed on the sentence that *explains* the bug. It now scans only ```powershell blocks --
every `--user-data-dir` inside a command must be followed by `=`. A pin that matches prose will match
tomorrow's prose. Lane: 12 passed, including the in-lane scoped typecheck on the restored tree.
